"""Backcountry.com category inventory (issue #222).

Backcountry is a Next.js storefront.  The global header ships the full shop
taxonomy inside the same hydration payload the PLP uses:

    https://www.backcountry.com/
        #__NEXT_DATA__.props.pageProps.targeters.headerNavigation[]

Each top-level targeter has ``title``, ``url`` and a ``content`` block; link
blocks live at ``content[type="links"].sections[]`` and each section exposes
``title``, an optional ``url`` and ``items[]`` of ``name``/``url``/``categoryId``.

Captured 2026-10-05 from the live homepage:

    * 14 top-level departments, 109 sections (12 departments / 60 sections carry
      links; "Guides" and the remaining sections are empty nav spacers)
    * 469 subcategory links
    * 398 distinct URLs -- the other 71 links are cross-listed under more than
      one department/section
    * 230 links carry a ``categoryId``; the rest (``/rc/`` redirect
      endpoints and brand-filtered URLs) are kept and crawled by URL only

The inventory below is the **normalized** form of that tree: every link is one
flat record with a stable ``slug`` of ``<department>/<section>/<name>`` derived
from its labels.  Those slugs are unique across the whole tree, so they are
usable directly as the spider's ``-a category=`` values and as anchors in the
README.  Nothing is dropped for lacking a ``categoryId`` -- Backcountry accepts
plain HTML pagination for ``/cat/``, ``/rc/`` and filtered brand URLs alike.
"""

